<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adi Shankara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Adi Shankaracharya, Ādi Śaṅkara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adi Shankara, also called Adi Shankaracharya, was an Indian Vedic scholar-monk, philosopher and teacher of Advaita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> who lived in the 8th century CE. He wrote commentaries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> sutras, ten principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, centred on the liberating knowledge that the individual self is Ātman-Brahman. Reliable information on his life is scant: the oldest hagiographies place his birth at Kaladi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, and the Shankara of legend who toured the subcontinent defeating opponents in debate and founding four mathas was created in the 14th century. Over 300 texts are attributed to him, most likely by others, and the title Shankaracharya derives from his name.</p></div>
